913:### Handling Failures by Kind
914-
915-A `try` has **one** `catch`. There is no second `catch` and no chain of handlers
916-to try in turn, but a handler can ask what kind of failure it caught and match on
917-it: `catch error of <Type>` runs its body only for the kinds `<Type>` names.
918-
919-```redblue
920-try
921-    set record to files.read("records/cleo.rb")
922-catch error of Io problem
923-    say "could not read the record: {problem.message}"
924-    give back
925-end
926-```
927-
928-A failure of any other kind is not handled here. A handler that does not match
929-does not run at all — the `finally` beside it is still owed — and the failure
930-belongs to whatever is written around this `try`: a `catch` there takes it, and a
931-program with nothing around it stops with it as its own.
932-
933-The list of names is closed, and it is short:
934-
935-| Name | The failures it matches |
936-|---|---|
937-| `Error` | every kind below — what an untyped `catch` matches, spelled as a match |
938-| `Runtime` | the program's own expression went wrong: a type mismatch, an index past the end of a list, a division by zero, a name that is not bound |
939-| `Io` | the host's filesystem and environment: a file that is not there, a path that cannot be read, a module that cannot be loaded |
940-| `Limit` | one of the host's resource limits: the step budget, the call-depth limit, or a loop's iteration cap |
941-
942-Any other name is refused by the parser, which quotes the name that was written
943-and the four that exist — `catch error of FileError` does not compile into a
944-handler that matches no failure at all. The host's other kinds, `Lexer`,
945-`Parser` and `Analyzer`, are not in the list: they are raised before the program
946-runs, so there is no `try` inside a program that could catch one.
947-
948-### The Caught Failure
949-
950-A handler that names a kind binds the failure, and the failure is a record of
951-four fields:
952-
953-| Field | What it holds |
954-|---|---|
955-| `kind` | the kind that was caught — `Runtime`, `Io` or `Limit` — even by a handler that matched `Error` |
956-| `message` | the failure's own message, which an untyped handler's `error` cannot give you |
957-| `line` | the line it happened on, or `0` for a failure with no position |
958-| `column` | the column it happened at, or `0` for a failure with no position |
959-
960-```redblue
961-try
962-    set sum to 1 + "a"
963-catch error of Runtime problem
964-    say "{problem.kind} on line {problem.line}"
965-    say problem.message
966-end
967-```
968-
969-```
970-Runtime on line 2
971-Cannot add non-numbers
972-```
973-
974-The failure is a value like any other: it goes into a list, into a record, and
975-back out of a function. `0` is a line no source file has, which is how a failure
976-with no position says so without a second spelling. `line` is the position to
977-read on both engines — a `.rbc` instruction carries a line and no column, so the
978-two place a failure at a different column by their own convention.
979-
980-The kind a failure was matched on is not the name it is *reported* under when
981-nothing caught it: an uncaught `Io` failure prints `IoError`, and an uncaught
982-`Limit` prints `RuntimeError`, because a limit is a runtime failure.
983-
